// Installed to /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/52unattended-upgrades-local on VM 103.
// Canonical copy: hermes-vm/etc/apt.conf.d/52unattended-upgrades-local in
// github.com/mnbf9rca/kubernetes_config
//
// A SEPARATE FILE, NOT AN EDIT TO 50unattended-upgrades. apt reads conf.d in
// lexical order, so 52 is read after 50 - and a `unattended-upgrades` package
// upgrade can then replace its own 50 file without a conffile prompt and
// without silently reverting any of this.
//
// SCALARS AND LISTS BEHAVE DIFFERENTLY, and getting this wrong is how a
// "security-only" policy quietly widens. For a SCALAR (Automatic-Reboot,
// Automatic-Reboot-Time) the last assignment wins, so 52 overrides 50. For a
// LIST - and Origins-Pattern is a list - a later block APPENDS to whatever 50
// already declared; it does not replace it. APT provides #clear for exactly
// this, and the block below uses it. Today the practical difference is nil,
// because Debian's shipped 50unattended-upgrades leaves only the same two
// Debian-Security patterns uncommented and the result would be a harmless
// duplicate. The reason to clear anyway is the future: a 50 file (or an
// operator edit) that enables -updates would NOT be overridden by this file,
// and automatic upgrades would silently stop being security-only while this
// comment insisted otherwise.
#clear Unattended-Upgrade::Origins-Pattern;

// Debian's security-only default, stated explicitly rather than inherited.
// The estate's other update surfaces (keel, Renovate, the update session) cover
// feature upgrades; this one exists for kernel and library CVEs.
Unattended-Upgrade::Origins-Pattern {
        "origin=Debian,codename=${distro_codename},label=Debian-Security";
        "origin=Debian,codename=${distro_codename}-security,label=Debian-Security";
};

// REBOOT IS ON, by operator decision, August 26, 2026. Revision 1 of the
// estate update strategy deferred the reboot to the session; the operator
// overruled. A kernel patch waiting weeks is the worse trade, and a bad
// comeback is caught twice - by hermes-app-alive, which runs at 05:45, ONE
// HOUR after this window, and by the hermes-update script's health assertion
// the next time it runs. The one-hour gap is the point: the reboot must have
// finished and the lingering user units must have come back before the check
// looks. (The continuous 15-minute monitor an earlier draft relied on here was
// deleted on 2026-08-26 as overengineering; detection latency is now up to
// about a day, accepted by the operator - "homelab not NASA".)
Unattended-Upgrade::Automatic-Reboot "true";
Unattended-Upgrade::Automatic-Reboot-WithUsers "true";

// 04:45 local time, and the VM's local time is UTC. The window is chosen to sit
// clear of everything the cluster does to this VM: the hermes-pull backup SSHes
// in at 02:00 UTC and the restic sweep runs at 03:00 UTC. The apt-daily-upgrade
// timer is moved to 04:00 (+/- 20 min) by the drop-in beside this file, so the
// upgrade has finished before the reboot fires.
Unattended-Upgrade::Automatic-Reboot-Time "04:45";

// Keep /boot from filling, which is the commonest way automatic kernel updates
// stop working silently.
Unattended-Upgrade::Remove-Unused-Kernel-Packages "true";
Unattended-Upgrade::Remove-New-Unused-Dependencies "true";

// The VM has no MTA, so mail would be a silent failure. The journal is the
// record, and the alarm is the hermes-update script's 14-day staleness gate on
// the /var/lib/apt/periodic/unattended-upgrades-stamp file that
// `unattended-upgrade` writes for itself in write_stamp_file().
Unattended-Upgrade::SyslogEnable "true";
